        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Deck first, tension second, motor
            last.</strong> A belt that sags under your foot is friction far more
            often than slack, and tightening it hides the symptom while adding to
            the load that caused it. Ten minutes with a bottle of silicone settles
            most of these.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            If lubrication and tension both check out and the belt still sags, look
            at heat and motor sizing using the
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">horsepower guide</a>
            — and until it is fixed, walk rather than run. Everything else lives in
            the
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">troubleshooting guide</a>.
          </p>
